Chapter: MatchArmsPerLine
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 BACKLOG 2.25. A `when` keeps every `is` arm written on a line, however
 the arms are packed across lines.

 The parser accepts an arm that starts on the same line as the arm before
 it, or at the column the arms are aligned on. `ln` used to be pinned to
 the line the match STARTED on and never advanced, so `tok.line == ln`
 could not hold again once the arms reached a second line -- and the arm
 that matched neither test ended the match in silence.

 The measured shape was not the "one arm per line" the entry described.
 Every arm on the FIRST line parsed, the FIRST arm of the second line
 parsed, and the second arm of the second line ended the `when` -- taking
 every later line with it. `many-per-line` below is that exact case: it
 kept `is 1`, `is 2`, `is 3` and silently dropped `is 4`, `is 5`, `is 6`
 AND the `is otherwise`, with no diagnostic of any kind.

 Both directions are pinned here because the two lines fail differently.
 `one-line` always worked (its arms share the match's own line) and must
 keep working. `many-per-line` is the regression, and its `is otherwise`
 matters most: a dropped catch-all leaves a `when` with no arm for its
 own default, and on an open type like Integer nothing is checking.

 Exhaustiveness was the only thing that ever caught this, and only on a
 closed variant -- which is why the one site anybody found (`Dev`'s
 GamepadManager, CL 8333) was a closed variant and everything else went
 unnoticed. This test uses Integer on purpose: no exhaustiveness check
 can stand in for the parser here.

Section: Arms

  one-line : Integer -> Text
  one-line (n) =
    when n
      is 1 -> "one" is 2 -> "two" is 3 -> "three"
      is otherwise -> "other"

  many-per-line : Integer -> Text
  many-per-line (n) =
    when n
      is 1 -> "one" is 2 -> "two"
      is 3 -> "three" is 4 -> "four"
      is 5 -> "five" is 6 -> "six"
      is otherwise -> "other"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("one-line: " & one-line 1 & one-line 2 & one-line 3 & one-line 9)
    print-line-uni ("many-1: " & many-per-line 1)
    print-line-uni ("many-2: " & many-per-line 2)
    print-line-uni ("many-3: " & many-per-line 3)
    print-line-uni ("many-4: " & many-per-line 4)
    print-line-uni ("many-5: " & many-per-line 5)
    print-line-uni ("many-6: " & many-per-line 6)
    print-line-uni ("many-9: " & many-per-line 9)
  end
